        <p class="dates">
          <span>shipped 15 april 2026</span>
          <span class="sep">/</span>
          <span>overwritten 16 september 2026</span>
        </p>
      </header>

      <section class="prose">
        <p>
          I shipped this page in April and I can't read the top of it now
          without wincing. Some of that is the prose, which was doing a
          sales job on a reader I hadn't met and didn't need to convince.
          But the prose isn't the interesting failure. Underneath it the
          page makes a specific argument about how to work with a language
          model, and six months of releases took that argument apart in a
          way I didn't see coming — not by proving it wrong, exactly, but
          by making most of it somebody else's problem.
        </p>

        <p>
          So this page is a <Gloss
            def="A manuscript page that has been scraped down and written over, where the earlier text remains faintly legible beneath the later one. The term survives because the erasure was never complete enough to be an erasure."
            >palimpsest</Gloss
          > now. The April argument is still here, in its own hand, dimmed
          but intact. September is written over and around it. Nothing has
          been deleted, and there's a control at the bottom of the next
          section that will hand you the original clean if you'd rather
          read what actually shipped than what I think of it.
        </p>

        <Marginal>
          Keeping the old copy is not sentiment. It's the only fixed
          reference I have to measure six months of my own drift against,
          which is the argument this whole page ends up making about
          everything else.
        </Marginal>
      </section>

      <section id="april" class="prose">
        <h2>What it said</h2>

        <p>Here is how the page opened in April.</p>

        <div class="april-layer">
          <p class="stamp">15 april 2026</p>
          <p class="april-hed">
            Have one conversation. Walk away with an LLM calibrated to how
            you actually think.
          </p>
          <p class="april-deck">
            Not by learning prompts — by teaching it you. Twenty minutes,
            any LLM with a decent context window, no tools required.
          </p>
        </div>

        <Marginal>
          Every section had a little lowercase kicker over it. "an
          exercise." "before anything else." "for the obsessives." I
          thought that was voice. It was a funnel wearing a cardigan.
        </Marginal>

        <p>
          And here is the sentence the whole thing rested on, from the
          closing instructions in the prompt itself — the part that told
          the model what to produce at the end:
        </p>

        <div class="april-layer">
          <p class="stamp">15 april 2026</p>
          <p class="april-quote">
            Draft a system prompt that encodes this calibration —
            something I could paste into future conversations
            <em>so we don't start from zero.</em>
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          That last clause is the bet, and it's the clause that lost.
        </p>

        <details class="retrieve">
          <summary>read the April page as it shipped</summary>
          <div class="retrieved">
            <p class="retrieved-note">
              Unedited, as published 15 April 2026. Kept whole rather than
              corrected, for reasons the rest of the page gets to.
            </p>

            <h3>The promise</h3>
            <p>
              Have one conversation. Walk away with an LLM calibrated to
              how you actually think. Not by learning prompts — by
              teaching it you. Twenty minutes, any LLM with a decent
              context window, no tools required.
            </p>

            <h3>A question first</h3>
            <p>
              What's the last thing you changed your mind about? Sit with
              it for ten seconds. Don't scroll.
            </p>
            <p>
              That's the shape of an opener. Anyone can answer it in one
              breath. But notice what you just did — you reached for a
              specific domain (tech? politics? a person?), you hesitated
              at a particular spot, maybe you hedged a word. The protocol
              below is twenty minutes of questions like that, with an
              agent watching which domain you reached for, how long you
              hesitated, whether you hedged — and pulling the thread. The
              unlock isn't the question. It's the follow-up.
            </p>

            <h3>The receipts</h3>
            <p>
              Two short exchanges. Same opening intent, different shape.
              Read both.
            </p>
            <p class="retrieved-label">
              what you've had a thousand times
            </p>
            <p class="retrieved-dialogue">
              <b>agent:</b> Welcome! I'm excited to get to know you. Let's
              start with something simple — tell me about yourself.<br />
              <b>you:</b> I work in software. Side projects in generative
              art and ML.<br />
              <b>agent:</b> Wonderful! What draws you to generative art?<br
              />
              <b>you:</b> The overlap of code and aesthetics.<br />
              <b>agent:</b> Beautiful. And your ML work?<br />
              <b>you:</b> Thinking about memory systems.<br />
              <b>agent:</b> Memory systems are such a rich area. What makes
              them interesting to you?
            </p>
            <p>
              You are Googling yourself. The agent is transcribing. Nobody
              has learned anything.
            </p>

            <h3>What this is not</h3>
            <p>
              Not a personality quiz. Not a coaching intake. Not a vibe
              check. Not onboarding for an AI assistant. It's
              epistemological infrastructure — building the shared
              language and mutual model that lets two minds, one
              biological and one artificial, do better work together than
              either could alone. The eureka isn't "wow, the AI knows me."
              It's "wow, I said something I didn't know I thought, and now
              we have a word for it."
            </p>

            <h3>Why it's built this way</h3>
            <p>
              <b>Low floor, high ceiling.</b> Every opener is answerable in
              one breath. The agent's follow-up transforms a casual answer
              into self-discovery. You never feel tested; the depth
              emerges, it isn't assigned.
            </p>
            <p>
              <b>Opt-out is data.</b> Skips, hedges, and "pass" responses
              are first-class signal. They map boundaries, trust
              thresholds, and protected territory. Chasing a skip is a
              trust violation.
            </p>
            <p>
              <b>Earn the hard questions.</b> Provocative moves — forced
              tradeoffs, surfacing contradictions, kill-your-darlings
              scenarios — come after rapport, not before. The progression
              is coffee, then sparring, then collaboration.
            </p>
            <p>
              <b>Register evolves.</b> The agent's vocabulary and tone are
              a live instrument, not a fixed setting. By the end, the
              conversation should sound like two people deep in the same
              discipline — because it is.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </section>

      <section id="months" class="prose">
        <h2>What six months did</h2>

        <p>
          The bet was that a model's picture of you was scarce and
          perishable. That every conversation started cold, that the work
          of establishing how you think had to be redone each time, and
          that the way out was to do that work once, deliberately, and
          carry the result forward as a piece of text you owned.
        </p>

        <p>
          Every clause of that has been overtaken. Memory shipped, and it
          shipped well enough that profiles accumulate whether you run an
          exercise or not. The durable part of calibration moved out of the
          paste buffer and into the harness — into files that sit in a
          repository and load themselves, scoped to a directory, so that
          the same model reads you differently depending on where you
          launched it from. And the surfaces multiplied. A chat window, a
          coworking surface, a terminal, a raw API call: the same weights
          underneath, but a different house voice, a different memory
          substrate, different defaults about how much to say and how hard
          to push. There is no longer a single calibration of you to hold,
          which means there is no longer a single artifact that could hold
          it.
        </p>

        <Marginal>
          The per-directory thing is the part that surprised me most. The
          unit of personalization quietly stopped being the person and
          became the working context. I am a different collaborator in one
          repository than in another, and that turns out to be correct.
        </Marginal>

        <p>
          That isn't a small correction to the April page. It's the removal
          of its reason to exist. If the platforms accumulate a profile for
          free, and the harness holds the durable part in a file that loads
          itself, then a twenty-minute conversation whose output is a
          paste-forward system prompt is solving a problem that has been
          solved around it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="hearth" class="prose">
        <h2>The furnace and the hearth</h2>

        <p>
          <Gloss
            def="Albert Borgmann, philosopher of technology. His Technology and the Character of Contemporary Life (1984) introduces the device paradigm and the counterweight he calls focal practice."
            >Borgmann</Gloss
          > has the distinction I needed and didn't have in April. A
          <Gloss
            def="Borgmann's term for technology that delivers a commodity while withdrawing its machinery and its demands from view. The commodity becomes instantly and effortlessly available; the practice that used to produce it disappears."
            >device</Gloss
          > delivers a commodity with its machinery hidden and its demands
          on you reduced to nothing. The furnace delivers warmth, and you
          never touch a log, never learn which wood burns slow, never plan
          an afternoon around it. A focal practice delivers the same
          commodity worse and something else well. A hearth is a bad way to
          heat a house. Nobody seriously argues otherwise. It survives,
          where it survives, because the splitting and the tending and the
          sitting-around-it are the point, and none of that comes out of a
          vent.
        </p>

        <p>
          Automatic memory is the furnace. It is better than my exercise at
          the thing my exercise claimed to do, and it ought to be — that is
          what devices are for, and resenting it would be strange. Where
          the April page went wrong was in arguing that the exercise was a
          better furnace.
        </p>

        <Pull
          >The April page argued the exercise was a better furnace. It was
          a hearth, described in the vocabulary of heating.</Pull
        >

        <p>
          What the hearth actually produces here is twenty minutes of being
          asked questions by something paying close attention that has no
          stake in flattering you. The output of that session was never the
          system prompt. It was the part where I said something out loud
          and then had to sit with the fact that I'd said it. That product
          is substrate-independent. It does not improve when the model
          improves and it does not arrive when the memory becomes
          automatic, because it was never the model's to deliver.
        </p>

        <Marginal>
          Borgmann is often read as saying focal practices are morally
          better than devices. He isn't quite saying that and I'm
          definitely not. Most devices are a mercy. The claim is narrower:
          a device that replaces a practice delivers the commodity and
          drops whatever else the practice was doing, and it's worth
          knowing which of those you were actually there for.
        </Marginal>
      </section>

      <section id="field" class="prose">
        <h2>The field block</h2>

        <p>
          There's a second argument, and this one is a live claim rather
          than a reframe.
        </p>

        <p>
          A vine nursery keeps a <Gloss
            def="An isolated, virus-tested block of source material held so that every cutting downstream can be traced to something provably clean. Nurseries propagate from the block, never from a customer's field."
            >foundation block</Gloss
          >: dated, tested stock that exists for no commercial reason
          except to be the thing everything else gets re-derived from. You
          never take cuttings from the field population, however good it
          looks, because the field has been propagating from itself and the
          drift compounds silently. Automatic memory, as currently built,
          is a field population with no foundation block behind it. What
          persists between your sessions is usually not the things you
          said; it's a summary, and the next summary gets written with the
          previous summary in context. Each pass re-estimates who you are
          partly from the prior estimate rather than from the source.
        </p>

        <p>
          That is the shape that eats the tails. The rare thing you said
          once, the position you hold weakly, the domain you're fluent in
          but rarely raise — those are exactly what a summarizer drops
          first, and once dropped there is no mechanism in the loop that
          puts them back, because nothing in the loop is reading the
          original anymore. The nursery's answer to this is not cleverness,
          it's discipline. Accumulate, don't replace. Keep the dated
          reservoir, propagate from it rather than from the field, and
          assume the working population has drifted until you've checked.
        </p>

        <Marginal>
          Worked out at length in <a href="/essays/foundation-block"
            >the foundation block</a
          >, which is about model collapse in training corpora rather than
          in a profile of a person. Same ratchet, smaller blast radius.
        </Marginal>

        <p>
          Which gives the April artifact a job after all, just not the job
          it was written for. Not the thing you paste at the start of a
          conversation — the platforms do that now, and better. The dated
          reservoir you check the drifting profile against. Ask the model
          what it thinks you believe. Read the answer next to something you
          actually wrote before it started watching. Where those two
          disagree, the profile has drifted, and you are the only party in
          the loop in a position to notice.
        </p>

        <p>
          The obvious response to drift is to keep more of everything.
          Longer memory, bigger profile, more context. That doesn't work
          the way it sounds like it should, because attention mass is
          conserved — the scores get normalized to sum to one no matter how
          many tokens are competing for the total. A profile grown to tens
          of kilobytes of accumulated session summaries is not a richer
          model of you. It's the same fixed quantity spread thinner, and
          the material in the middle of it gets the least. A longer memory
          is a bigger room with the same watt in it. What you want is not
          more of the profile retained but a smaller, denser one that has
          been curated against a reservoir, which is again the practice and
          not the device.
        </p>

        <Marginal>
          The acoustics of that is <a href="/essays/reverberant-middle"
            >the reverberant middle</a
          >. Short version: the ends of a long context survive by two
          different mechanisms and the middle isn't quiet, it's been
          reflected until it forgot which wall it came from.
        </Marginal>
      </section>

      <section id="yield" class="prose">
        <h2>Surprise yield</h2>

        <p>
          If this is going to be a claim rather than a mood, it should name
          a number and then risk it.
        </p>

        <p>
          The measure I'd use is surprise yield: of the things a model
          tells you about yourself at the end of a session, what fraction
          could you not have written down beforehand? Not whether it was
          accurate. Accuracy is cheap and a mirror scores perfectly on it.
          The question is whether anything came back that you didn't put
          in.
        </p>

        <p>
          And here is the part that makes it worth measuring instead of
          just saying. Automatic memory drives surprise yield toward zero
          by construction. It is assembled from what you have already said.
          It can be more comprehensive than you, faster than you, better
          organized than you, and it still cannot hand you a thing you
          never gave it.
        </p>

        <Pull
          >The furnace can only hand back what you fed it. That isn't a
          defect. That's the definition.</Pull
        >

        <p>
          Which predicts something checkable, on yourself, this week. Run
          the exercise cold against a model that knows nothing about you.
          Run it again on a surface where six months of memory is loaded.
          If the second yields more surprise, I'm wrong about the mechanism
          and the device is doing more than I'm crediting it for. My
          prediction is that it yields less, and that the gap widens the
          longer the memory has been accumulating — because the better the
          profile gets at anticipating you, the more efficiently it routes
          the conversation back through ground you've already covered.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="breaks" class="prose">
        <h2>Where this breaks</h2>

        <p>
          I owe you the places it doesn't hold, because a frame that only
          confirms is worth nothing.
        </p>

        <p>
          The first is that I may be marking my own homework. I wrote an
          essay in September about clonal degeneration and foundation
          blocks, and then turned around and found the same shape in my own
          memory tooling a week later. That is either the correspondence
          being real or it is me holding a hammer, and I can't fully
          separate those from the inside. The honest account: I noticed the
          drift first — a model telling me with real confidence that I
          cared about something I had mentioned exactly once, in passing,
          months earlier — and went looking for the vocabulary afterward.
          That's the right order to have found it in. Weigh it knowing I
          have a stake in the story holding together.
        </p>

        <p>
          The second is sharper and I can't resolve it. The collapse
          argument assumes the memory loop re-summarizes from summaries. If
          a system retains source material and re-derives the profile from
          originals each time, there's an outcross in the loop, the ratchet
          never clicks, and my entire second argument evaporates. From
          outside, I don't know which of those any given product is doing.
          Neither do you. Building a practice on that ignorance is
          uncomfortable, and I'd rather say plainly that the practice is
          partly insurance against not knowing than pretend I've measured
          something I haven't.
        </p>

        <p>
          The third is that hearths do sometimes just lose, and pretending
          otherwise is nostalgia with a reading list. Some practices
          deserve to be replaced and the people defending them are usually
          fluent about why they shouldn't be. Whether this one is a hearth
          or a buggy whip is not something I get to settle by asserting it
          in the same essay where I introduced the distinction.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="prompt" class="prose">
        <h2>The prompt</h2>

        <p>
          It's still here and it still works, but I want to be careful
          about what "works" is now claiming. It's a piece of text that
          makes a capable model run a particular kind of conversation. It
          has no special status. A current model in a decent harness
          already does a fair amount of what it asks — matching your
          register, noticing a contradiction, declining to flatter —
          without being told, which means some meaningful fraction of the
          instructions below are redundant scaffolding I've left standing
          because cutting them properly is a separate afternoon and I'd
          rather show you the artifact as it actually is.
        </p>

        <p>
          Use it as a starting point and expect to cut it. Yours should not
          end up looking like mine.
        </p>

        <div class="prompt-block">
          <button class="copy" type="button" data-copy-target="prompt">
            <span class="copy-label">copy</span>
            <span class="copy-done" aria-hidden="true">copied</span>
          </button>
          <pre id="prompt-text"><code>{prompt}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="where" class="prose">
        <h2>Where you are</h2>

        <p>
          One last thing, and it's the reason this page isn't a funnel.
          <Gloss
            def="William Gibson, attributed in various forms from the late 1990s onward. He has said he does not remember first saying it, which is somehow appropriate."
            >Gibson's line</Gloss
          > — the future is already here, it's just not evenly distributed
          — gets quoted as though it's about early adopters being ahead.
          The useful reading is narrower. At any moment the people you're
          talking to are standing in genuinely different places with
          different tools in reach, and advice pitched at the middle of
          that distribution is wrong for nearly everyone in it.
        </p>

        <p>
          So: if you've never done anything like this, if your relationship
          with these tools is a box you type into and close, then the
          exercise is worth twenty minutes on its own terms and you should
          disregard everything above about drift and reservoirs. That's a
          problem you don't have yet, and borrowing it early just makes the
          thing feel like homework.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you have memory switched on and a profile that's been
          accumulating for months, the exercise is less interesting to you
          than an audit. Ask the thing what it believes about you. Read
          that next to something you wrote before it started watching. The
          gap is the finding, and it's usually more specific and stranger
          than you expect.
        </p>

        <p>
          And if you're already keeping harness files — if there's a
          config in the repository you're working in, and you've noticed
          the model reads you differently depending on which directory you
          launched from — then this stopped being a question about a prompt
          a while ago and became a question about where the canonical copy
          lives and what re-anchors it. I don't have a clean answer. I have
          a file, a habit of reading it next to what the model says about
          me, and a suspicion that this is where the next version of this
          page comes from.
        </p>

        <p>
          Which is the honest place to stop. This page was wrong in April
          in a way I couldn't see until September, and it's presumably
          wrong now in a way I won't see until spring. That's the argument
          for keeping both layers rather than shipping a confident
          replacement. Not humility, exactly. Just the recognition that the
          old copy is the only thing I'll have to measure the drift
          against.
        </p>
      </section>

      <Backlinks slug="calibrate" />
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<script>
  // copy-to-clipboard for any button[data-copy-target]
  document
    .querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-copy-target]')
    .forEach((btn) => {
      btn.addEventListener('click', async () => {
        const target = document.getElementById('prompt-text');
        if (!target) return;
        const text = target.innerText;
        try {
          await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);
          btn.classList.add('is-done');
          setTimeout(() => btn.classList.remove('is-done'), 1600);
        } catch {
          const range = document.createRange();
          range.selectNodeContents(target);
          const sel = window.getSelection();
          sel?.removeAllRanges();
          sel?.addRange(range);
        }
      });
    });

  // scroll-synced table of contents
  const links = Array.from(
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>('[data-toc]'),
  );
  const sections = links
    .map((a) => document.querySelector(a.getAttribute('href') || ''))
    .filter((el): el is Element => Boolean(el));

  if (sections.length > 0 && 'IntersectionObserver' in window) {
    const seen = new Map<Element, number>();
    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
      (entries) => {
        entries.forEach((entry) => {
          seen.set(entry.target, entry.intersectionRatio);
        });
        let best: Element | null = null;
        let bestRatio = 0;
        seen.forEach((ratio, el) => {
          if (ratio > bestRatio) {
            bestRatio = ratio;
            best = el;
          }
        });
        if (!best) return;
        const id = (best as Element).id;
        links.forEach((a) => {
          a.classList.toggle(
            'is-active',
            a.getAttribute('href') === `#${id}`,
          );
        });
      },
      { rootMargin: '-10% 0px -70% 0px', threshold: [0, 0.25, 0.5, 1] },
    );
    sections.forEach((el) => observer.observe(el));
  }
</script>

